# The STAMPED accessibility scene: the assertion the a11y milestone's
# 719 legs never made. Every widget the a11y scene asserts is built
# LIVE; the two entries here are COPIES, stamped from a collection row's
# template, and their labels come from each row's OWN field (the
# template's a11y_label is element-sourced). A list row announcing its
# own name to assistive tech is the whole point of a sourced template
# label, and until this scene nothing had ever read a stamped copy's
# accessibility surface from the platform's real tree.
#
# ITS OWN SCENE, AND THE REASON IS SCENE SHAPE, NOT SIZE. A For
# materializes as a COLUMN, harness registries are creation-order, and
# container creation order differs by language (statement-shaped
# construction is parent-first, expression trees are children-first —
# guests/haskell/reorder.hs documents the rule). The a11y scene asserts
# every container kind ORDINALLY, so a For added anywhere inside it
# makes `column#0` name different widgets in different languages. This
# scene asserts no container at all, so the For's column can land at
# either end of the registry and every target below still means the
# same thing everywhere.
#
# THE IDS ARE ELEMENT-SOURCED like the labels, and the first run is
# why: expect_ax resolves its target to the node's authored identifier
# and then searches the REAL tree by that identifier, so copies sharing
# a const template id are indistinguishable to it — and the read now
# REFUSES an ambiguous id with the measured count rather than answering
# with whichever element it found first, which is exactly what it did
# when these assertions were first run against a shared id (the first
# copy's label came back as the second's, under a correct-looking
# sentence). A shared const id remains LEGAL — nothing in the core
# deduplicates — it is just not a thing this verb can read back.
expect_ax entry#0 "field/First note"
expect_ax entry#last "field/Second note"
